WEBVTT

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:07.241
Weehawken, New Jersey. The
Vice President, Aaron Burr,

00:00:07.241 --> 00:00:10.526
and Alexander Hamilton stand ten paces apart.

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:17.579
Hamilton was mortally
wounded. He died the next day.

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:20.242
The news spread fast.

00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:27.153
In New York a coroner's jury was
sitting. Its object, Burr wrote,

00:00:27.153 --> 00:00:32.827
was to obtain an inquest of murder. So he
wrote to his son-in-law, Joseph Alston,

00:00:32.827 --> 00:00:35.418
and put the dangerous part in cipher.

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:39.391
The letter survives in the
Gilder Lehrman Collection.

00:00:39.391 --> 00:00:42.644
Its note says the cipher
has never been decoded.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:49.778
132 signs: figures, and little bundles of
hatched strokes. One sign for each letter,

00:00:49.778 --> 00:00:51.796
and the words kept apart.

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:56.833
Short words give themselves away.
Three signs that fit: “the”. After,

00:00:56.833 --> 00:01:00.728
being about 20,000, three words
that fit: “in this state”.

00:01:00.728 --> 00:01:04.623
The same four signs twice,
before a question mark: “what”.

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:11.955
The key follows at once, and it is built
in runs: figures counting down, then up,

00:01:11.955 --> 00:01:13.944
then Roman numerals in order.

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:18.983
Every sign reads.

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:41.960
The collection's transcript had dropped
two words. The page has them: “murder be”.

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:46.971
Burr: “If the inquest of murder be
found, what would you advise? To stand?

00:01:46.971 --> 00:01:48.648
To go to Europe, or what?”

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:55.364
The jury returned wilful murder on the
second of August. Burr had already gone.

00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:02.635
A week after the duel, Burr asked in
cipher whether to run. 222 years later,

00:02:02.635 --> 00:02:03.866
it can be read.

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:12.640
Four years later, Burr had been
tried for treason and acquitted.

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:17.081
In February 1808 he was hiding
in Baltimore, under another name,

00:02:17.081 --> 00:02:19.717
waiting for money to sail for England.

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:25.251
He wrote to his daughter Theodosia,
at The Oaks in South Carolina,

00:02:25.251 --> 00:02:29.307
in another cipher: nothing
but numbers, from 1 to 26.

00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:34.827
The collection files these sheets as
two more pages of the 1804 letter.

00:02:34.827 --> 00:02:37.872
They are another cipher, from another year.

00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:42.885
One word comes twice running, with
the same six numbers both times.

00:02:43.300 --> 00:02:48.930
Count how often numbers repeat at each
spacing. Six stands out, and twelve.

00:02:48.930 --> 00:02:51.032
Six alphabets, used in turn.

00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:56.141
Line up each alphabet with English,
and the six shifts fall into place.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:03.569
Burr: “We must submit, submit,
to ten days more of suspense.”

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:13.699
Burr: “No person must suspect that I mean
to visit you in S.C. Let it be believed,

00:03:13.699 --> 00:03:16.897
except by mari, that I am in Philadelphia.”

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:23.167
He sailed for England on the seventh of
June. Both of his ciphers can now be read,

00:03:23.167 --> 00:03:24.728
from the ciphers alone.

